<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores facts in tables, relates them through values and answers questions about them. It can also refuse changes that break rules you have given it, and make several changes succeed or fail together. That combination is useful whenever several people or processes depend on the same facts.</p>
<p>A shop is an ordinary example. Mina places an order containing a mug and two packets of tea. The shop needs to know who ordered, what was promised, how much stock remains and whether payment succeeded. Looking up the order is easy. Keeping those answers correct while requests overlap, connections break and software changes is the larger job.</p>
<p>This article follows that shop far enough to give you a working model of the whole job. The linked articles develop each decision when you need it. Read a task’s main article through before taking its deeper links: it supplies a usable first case, and its children investigate particular questions. The <a href="#choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">route table at the end</a> gives a starting order for a first application and shorter entrances for an existing problem.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id, title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="one-action-changes-several-facts">One action changes several facts</h2>
<p>Suppose there is one mug left. Accepting Mina’s next order must record the order, record its mug item and reduce available stock from one to zero. If the application writes those facts separately and stops after the second write, the order has an item but the mug is still available to sell. Changing the order of the writes merely changes which partial result can remain.</p>
<p>A database gives these related facts a shared home. All application instances ask it for the stock and send their changes to it. It checks specified rules and coordinates competing changes. The application can ask it to treat the three writes as one <strong>transaction</strong>, whose committed effect is all or nothing.</p>
<p>That does not tell the database everything the business means. A rule that available stock cannot be negative is different from a rule that every accepted order has reserved its items. A database can enforce the first with a check on one value. The second also needs a correctly written ordering procedure. If nobody expresses that rule, storing the data in a relational database will not invent it.</p>
<p>Payment adds a boundary. The shop’s database can record a payment attempt, but a payment provider has its own records and can decline or fail to answer. The database cannot undo a charge at that provider just by rolling back its own transaction. The shop therefore needs explicit states, such as waiting for payment, paid and cancelled, and a way to discover what happened after an interrupted attempt.</p>

<h2 id="tables-describe-facts-and-relationships">Tables describe facts and relationships</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> gives one kind of fact a structure. Its named <strong>columns</strong> hold values of declared types; each <strong>row</strong> is one instance. In this small shop, one customer row identifies a customer, one order row identifies an order and one item row says how much of a product that order contains.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The shop’s four tables"><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>One row means</th><th>Example values</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>customers</code></td><td>One customer</td><td><code>customer_id = 1</code>, <code>name = 'Mina'</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>orders</code></td><td>One order belonging to one customer</td><td><code>order_id = 101</code>, <code>customer_id = 1</code>, <code>status = 'paid'</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>order_items</code></td><td>One product on one order</td><td><code>order_id = 101</code>, <code>product_id = 'TEA'</code>, <code>quantity = 2</code>, <code>unit_price_gbp = 8.00</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>products</code></td><td>One product and its available stock</td><td><code>product_id = 'MUG'</code>, <code>available = 1</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A <strong>primary key</strong> identifies a row uniquely and cannot be missing. Names are unsuitable customer keys because two customers can share a name and a customer can change it. This shop gives each customer a separate integer identifier. The order stores that identifier, rather than a fresh copy of the customer’s name.</p>
<p>The order’s <code>customer_id</code> is a <strong>foreign key</strong>: its value must identify an existing customer. This prevents an order referring to customer 999 when no such customer exists. It does not require every customer to have an order. The item table references both an order and a product. Its key is the pair <code>(order_id, product_id)</code>, because this shop combines repeated purchases of the same product into one quantity. Another shop might need separate lines for different discounts.</p>
<p>These are parts of the <strong>schema</strong>, the definition of the tables, types and rules. A quantity can be an integer with <code>NOT NULL</code> and <code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code>. A price here is an exact decimal number of pounds, <code>numeric(10,2)</code>, rather than an unexplained floating-point value. The purchased price belongs on the item: changing tomorrow’s catalogue price should not change yesterday’s order.</p>
<p>A column can permit <code>NULL</code>, SQL’s marker for a missing value. Here an order’s <code>shipped_at</code> is NULL until shipment is recorded. It is neither a date nor the text “NULL”. Decide what absence means before allowing it; “not shipped” and “shipment time unknown” may need different representation if the business distinguishes them.</p>
<p>The schema describes the logical facts. The database’s files, pages and indexes are their physical representation. You can change an index to make a lookup cheaper without changing what an order means. You can also combine tables when reading them, so separating customer and order facts does not force the application to assemble every answer itself.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a> takes requirements through identities, relationships, values and enforceable rules, then tests the design against reads, changes and deletion. Read it before choosing specialised value or constraint designs.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-a-result-then-check-what-it-means">Ask for a result, then check what it means</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language most relational systems use to define, read and change data. A <code>SELECT</code> asks for a result. This one asks for Mina’s order identifiers and statuses, in identifier order:</p>
<Sql code={selectOrders} label="Mina’s orders, using her customer identifier" />
<p><code>FROM</code> names the table, <code>WHERE</code> keeps rows meeting a condition, and <code>SELECT</code> chooses the columns to return. The fixture used here contains Mina’s paid order 101 and Arun’s empty draft 102, so this query returns one row: <code>101, paid</code>. <code>ORDER BY</code> makes the order of results part of the request. Without it, an order you happened to observe can change with the execution plan or stored data.</p>
<p>A <strong>join</strong> pairs rows whose values match. The next query connects each order to its customer and its item rows, then <code>GROUP BY</code> gathers the matches for each order. <code>SUM</code> adds quantity times the purchased price to make an item total. The short names <code>o</code>, <code>c</code> and <code>i</code> are aliases for the tables.</p>
<Sql code={summary} label="One item total for each order that has items" />
<p>The left table in Figure 1 already includes names from the customer join. Before playing, predict the number of rows after matching order 101 to its items. Then switch to <em>Keep: LEFT JOIN</em> and watch what happens to Arun’s empty order.</p>
<RowsFigure />
<p>The ordinary join returned order 101 twice, once for each product, before grouping reduced it to one row with £36. Counting the joined rows would count item matches, not orders. The repeated order identifier is expected: each result row represents a different order–item pair. Removing duplicates without deciding what one output row should mean can conceal a query mistake.</p>
<p>Order 102 disappeared because the ordinary join found no item for it. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> retains an unmatched order with NULLs on the item side. Its total is NULL, because there are no item values to add; displaying zero is a separate choice. Even then, <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts that retained row, while <code>COUNT(i.product_id)</code> counts only its non-null product values and returns zero. The query must answer the question you intended to ask.</p>
<p>NULL also changes filtering. <code>shipped_at = NULL</code> does not test for a missing shipment time: the comparison is unknown. Use <code>shipped_at IS NULL</code>. A <code>WHERE</code> condition retains rows only when it is true, so treating missing values as ordinary values can silently remove the rows you wanted.</p>
<p>The engine chooses how to obtain a requested result: which table to read first, how to match it and whether to use an index. That freedom lets the same SQL work as tables grow. It does not check the meaning of your question. SQL also has <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>; an update that succeeds can still change the wrong rows. Establish an expected answer on a small fixture, including an empty order and repeated matches, before trusting a plausible-looking result.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> supplies a disposable starting database and develops this process from first queries to substantial reads and changes. Complete its ordinary query path before following a deeper SQL technique.</p>

<h2 id="a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">A transaction protects a change within a stated contract</h2>
<p>Return to the one remaining mug, now for a new order 103. A transaction groups its order insertion, item insertion and stock reduction between <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code>. The writing transaction sees its own changes as it works. Other transactions do not see those unfinished changes in PostgreSQL. <code>COMMIT</code> keeps them together; <code>ROLLBACK</code> cancels them together.</p>
<p>Figure 2 starts with separate commits and a failed request. Play it, then choose <em>One transaction</em> and replay. Watch the new reader’s stock count beside its order and item.</p>
<OrderFigure />
<p>The failed transaction left no order and no item. The separate commits left both, with stock still available. On the successful transaction path, the new reader sees all three changes only after commit. This all-or-nothing effect is <strong>atomicity</strong>.</p>
<p>Now let Mina and Arun both try to buy the last mug. Each can read “one available” before either has committed. If both later write the value zero calculated in application memory, the final stock can be zero even though two orders were accepted. Putting each attempt in a transaction does not, by itself, prevent that mistake. <strong>Isolation</strong> specifies which interactions between overlapping transactions are permitted.</p>
<p>For this one-row stock rule, PostgreSQL can make the test and reduction one statement:</p>
<Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve one mug only while one is still available" />
<p>At PostgreSQL’s default <em>Read Committed</em> isolation, a competing updater waits if this row is already being changed, then rechecks the condition against the committed row. After one buyer reduces stock to zero, the other gets no returned row. The application must treat that as “no reservation”, roll back its attempted order and avoid claiming success. It commits the order and item only when the reservation returned one row. A non-negative stock constraint is an additional guard, not a replacement for checking the result.</p>
<p>That answers this small case. Rules involving several products or several rows need further reasoning about constraints, locks or stronger isolation. The familiar initials <strong>ACID</strong> gather atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. Consistency means preserving the rules of the system; the initials do not supply rules or make an incorrect transaction correct.</p>
<p><strong>Durability</strong> is the commit’s survival promise. Check what an acknowledged commit means for the chosen engine, settings, storage and failover arrangement. PostgreSQL normally waits for its local write-ahead log to be flushed, so it can reconstruct committed changes after a crash. Its asynchronous-commit option can acknowledge earlier and lose recent commits after a crash. A local durable commit alone says nothing about losing that storage device.</p>
<p>The payment provider is still outside this boundary. Commit the reservation and the intention to request payment together, then arrange payment and record its outcome through a recoverable application workflow. An interrupted or declined payment needs an explicit next action, such as checking the provider’s record or releasing the reservation. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops the transaction contract, concurrency choices and failure cases before its deeper investigations.</p>

<h2 id="the-application-is-part-of-the-database-system">The application is part of the database system</h2>
<p>A request reaches the database through a <strong>driver</strong>, a library that sends commands and converts returned values. With a database service, the driver uses a connection to the server. An embedded database instead runs within the application process. In either arrangement, the application must preserve the values and meaning of the operation at the boundary.</p>
<p>Pass Mina’s identifier as a bound parameter: for example, the PostgreSQL statement text can contain <code>WHERE customer_id = $1</code>, with the integer supplied separately. The driver’s binding API keeps a supplied value from becoming SQL syntax. Building SQL by joining user input into a string risks changing the command itself. Binding a customer identifier still does not establish that the signed-in user is entitled to read that customer’s orders; the application must check authority too.</p>
<p>A server application usually borrows connections from a bounded <strong>pool</strong>. One request owns its borrowed connection until its transaction has committed or rolled back. Sending <code>BEGIN</code> on one connection and the stock update on another does not create one transaction. Return the connection only after completing that work, and keep unrelated network calls and long waits outside the open transaction.</p>
<p>Some failures are known. A rejected constraint tells the application that the attempted change was refused. Others leave an unknown outcome. If the connection breaks while <code>COMMIT</code> is being processed, the database may have committed even though no success reply reached the driver. Repeating “create a new order and charge” from scratch can duplicate the effect.</p>
<p>Give a logical order attempt a stable identifier, enforce its uniqueness and make the result discoverable by that identifier. After a broken connection, check what is recorded before deciding to retry or reconcile the provider’s outcome. Use the provider’s corresponding duplicate-prevention mechanism where it offers one. A timeout bounds how long the caller waits; it does not prove that the remote work stopped.</p>
<p>Deadlines, connection limits and retry limits belong together. Retrying every slow request immediately can add load to an already overloaded database. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> follows one request through these boundaries, including driver values, connection ownership and recovery from interrupted work.</p>

<h2 id="correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">Correct SQL still has to do physical work</h2>
<p>To find order 101 without a useful access path, the engine can scan the orders table and test each row. An <strong>index</strong> stores a structure it can search by a value, then use to find the matching rows. A primary key commonly supplies such an index. An index on <code>orders.customer_id</code> may help find Mina’s orders as the table grows.</p>
<p>The benefit depends on the question. Finding a few orders can avoid most table reads. Reading nearly every order may be cheaper as a scan. Each extra index also takes space and adds work to changes that must maintain it. Joining tables, sorting results, retaining older row versions and sending a large answer all consume resources too.</p>
<p>A <strong>query plan</strong> shows the route the engine chose. PostgreSQL’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> reports the proposed work using estimates. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> executes the statement and adds observations, including rows and page accesses. It really performs data changes too, so investigate changing statements in an appropriate disposable environment.</p>
<p>For a slow order-history request, first record the SQL, parameter values, data size and observed delay. Inspect its measured plan: did it read thousands of rows to return ten, sort a large intermediate result or spend time waiting elsewhere? Form one explanation, such as a missing customer-and-date access path, and test a change on representative data. Compare the same correct result before and after, then check the effect on writes and other requests.</p>
<p>A fast isolated query is only one observation. Under load, requests share processor time, memory, storage, locks and connection slots. Averages can hide the slowest requests, so compare a high latency percentile, throughput and resource use at realistic concurrency. More hardware may help a resource limit; it will not repair a query that multiplies rows by mistake.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> supplies the full diagnosis-to-comparison method. Finish that method first. Its evidence then leads either to <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">query-plan diagnosis</a> for expensive query work or to <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a> for queueing and contention.</p>

<h2 id="data-and-applications-change-at-different-speeds">Data and applications change at different speeds</h2>
<p>The shop now needs a delivery instruction on every submitted order. Adding a required column immediately can break old application instances that do not send it. Existing rows also need an honest value: filling them all with an empty string may satisfy a database rule while hiding that the information was never collected.</p>
<p>Make the change in compatible stages. First add a representation that existing readers and writers can tolerate. Deploy code that handles old rows and supplies the new fact on the path where it becomes required. Convert existing data only where a valid value can be established, in bounded batches with progress recorded. Verify coverage and retire incompatible writers before strengthening the database rule.</p>
<p>Each stage needs a completion condition. “Deployment finished” is insufficient if an old worker still creates orders without the new field. Count the affected rows, identify all writers and check the actual running versions. The correct end rule may distinguish historical orders from newly submitted ones rather than pretend history has the new information.</p>
<p>The database also has to perform the schema or data work while serving traffic. Depending on the engine and operation, a change can scan or rewrite a table and hold locks. Try it on a representative copy, budget time and spare capacity, and decide how to stop or resume partial progress. Reverting application code is not recovery for data already deleted or transformed incorrectly.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> develops one complete change sequence, then routes to the deeper procedures for schema work, data jobs and database moves.</p>

<h2 id="operate-for-the-failure-you-must-recover-from">Operate for the failure you must recover from</h2>
<p>The database becomes a service people rely on. Give the application only the access it needs, protect credentials and network access, and assign ownership for maintenance and recovery. Watch request errors and latency alongside connection use, waiting transactions, storage growth and resource headroom. A service that is almost full during normal running may have no room for a restore, an index build or a traffic surge.</p>
<p>Two failures need different recovery mechanisms. If the server fails, a suitably maintained <strong>replica</strong>, another copy receiving changes, may allow the service to continue after failover. Whether every acknowledged order survives depends on what reached the replica and what the commit waited for. Whether a replica read sees a recent order also depends on how far it has applied changes.</p>
<p>If an authorised user commits a mistaken deletion, replication usually copies the deletion. A second current copy is not the retained earlier data you need. A <strong>backup</strong> and, where supported, retained change history can let you restore an older state or recover to a point before the mistake. Recovering a subset without losing later valid orders then requires careful validation and reconciliation.</p>
<p>State the required outcome in numbers: how much committed data may be lost, and how long correct service may be unavailable. These are the recovery point and recovery time objectives. They are requirements to test. A successful backup job establishes that a job completed; a timed restore into an isolated environment, followed by application-level checks, shows whether its data, keys, configuration and destination capacity are sufficient.</p>
<p>For this shop, validation might establish that accepted orders still have their items, reservation records agree with stock, and payment references can be reconciled with the provider. An available server that serves wrong data has not completed recovery. Keep traffic restricted until the checks required by the incident have passed.</p>
<p>A managed service changes who performs parts of the work. The shop still needs to know the provider’s retention and restore limits, its own remaining duties and who can initiate recovery. <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> connects access, observation, maintenance and incidents before its contextual operating procedures.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">Choose a system by testing the contract you need</h2>
<p>The shop has related facts, integrity rules, transactional changes and questions that will evolve. Those make a relational system a useful candidate. Some workloads instead favour a specialised analytical, search or other store; adding one also adds synchronisation and recovery work. Start from the questions and promises, then choose the arrangement that can demonstrate them.</p>
<p>Separate an engine from its deployment. An embedded library such as SQLite runs with the application; a server such as PostgreSQL serves clients across connections; a distributed relational service coordinates data across machines. Sharing tables and SQL does not give those arrangements identical writer limits, isolation behaviour, latency or recovery.</p>
<p>Write a small selection brief: the stock rule, peak concurrent order attempts, acceptable response times, likely data growth, tolerated interruption and data loss, and the team’s ability to run the service. Turn hard requirements into rejection tests. Exercise the same schema, queries and competing changes through the intended driver and network path. Include recovery tests and total operating cost before accepting a candidate.</p>
<p>You do not need to complete that evaluation before writing your first query. Use the disposable PostgreSQL setup at the beginning of <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> as a practice environment. For an adoption decision, <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> compares contracts and keeps a compact PostgreSQL candidate record. Take its <a href="/review/relational-next/sqlite/">SQLite follow-up</a> only for an embedded candidate, or its <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">CockroachDB follow-up</a> after a concrete distributed requirement survives comparison with a simpler service.</p>
